00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:24.730
PROFESSOR: OK, a
recap of last lecture.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.310
We started to talk
about liquids.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.160
I said I couldn't deal
with the liquid using

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:37.990
the approach that we have been
using for dilute particles--

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:41.930
Boltzmann-based
equation, for example.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:48.070
And so the discussion on liquid
is incomplete and very short.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.200
We started on bulk liquids.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.450
We said a very useful
definition measure

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:58.240
is the radial distribution
function, which

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.800
actually measures the
structure of a liquid itself.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.130
And then we introduced the Van
der Waals equation of states.

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.230
Historically, it's
very important.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.830
And, of course, we know
the ideal gas equation,

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.500
but Van der Waals
equation of state

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.445
actually give you the
liquid and solid states--

00:01:21.445 --> 00:01:24.850
uh, no, the vapor and
liquid states and also

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:29.810
give you the transition
between the liquid to vapor.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.630
So vapor [INAUDIBLE],,
at least qualitatively.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.010
And then say in terms of
trading the transport,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.250
the liquid, their
approach in the past,

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.130
modifying the
Boltzmann equation.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:50.290
And this eventually was the,
say, Enskog for example.

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.530
[? Consider ?] rather
than Boltzmann equation

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.750
as a single-particle
distribution

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.600
function, two-particle
distribution function.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.250
That was the Enskog
approach, but as I mentioned,

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:06.750
was not very successful because
it can't really deal even with

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.974
a very complete Enskog equation,
which I didn't write down,

00:02:09.974 --> 00:02:13.410
you cannot deal with liquid.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:18.810
You can deal with approximate
[? treating ?] the denser gas,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.690
but it can't be
extended to liquid.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:27.120
So then we talk about
Einstein's approach.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.510
His proposed objective
was to actually determine

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.260
the size of molecules,
small particles.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.890
And he can save the
Brownian motion picture,

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:39.630
although there were experiments.

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.090
Brownian did experiments
long before Einstein,

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.050
but he didn't seem to realize.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.710
And so a lot of his work
was a [? gedunking, ?]

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.960
as he is good at.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.320
And he showed that the relation
between the diffusivity

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.030
and viscosity, diffusivity
of particles in the fluids

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.430
and the viscosity of the fluids.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:05.820
And then that's an example
of the fluctuation, which

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.990
is diffusion, and dissipation,
which is viscosity.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.540
It's an example of a
fluctuation-dissipation

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.190
theorem.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:20.520
And from the Einstein approach,
there was then an extension

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:24.010
to Langevin equation of motion.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:30.900
So it's essentially f equals
ma, except in the fourth term,

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.320
there are two terms.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:39.160
One is a random driving force,
and the other is a drag force.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.970
So when you have a
particle in a fluid moving,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.930
we know that [? picture ?]
is the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.592
But that's actually
very general.

00:03:47.592 --> 00:03:49.800
[? It's ?] [? basically ?]
drag force is proportional

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.150
to velocity.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:57.690
And I commented that even the
longevity equation was not

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:02.190
quite right the way it's written
because it didn't consider

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:08.400
this time, the finite
time response when you

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.050
use the [? stock ?] solution.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.050
If you actually write down
the time response of the

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.090
[? stocks, ?] you
could see a solution,

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:18.370
you actually get an integral
differential equation.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:21.765
So you've seen integral
question quite a few times.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:31.010
We will come back
to continue some

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:35.540
of this discussion
in the last lecture,

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:41.900
but then we turn our attention
to deal with surfaces

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.650
and [? say ?]
interface in liquids.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:50.000
And we started with
potentials and the forces.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.010
So what's the most
[? important? ?]

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.430
In fact, I think
somewhere I said

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:58.070
before, if you
understand the energy,

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:02.210
you understand momentum, you
understand the second entropy,

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:07.400
you understand at least,
say, all the mechanical work.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:13.580
And the f equals,
say, the force itself,

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.290
is the gradient
of the potential.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:20.790
So we started
discussing the force.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:24.440
Particularly, we focused on
the Coulombic force, the force

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.470
interaction due to the charge.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.880
We cannot easily deal with
the covalent force, right,

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:36.560
where you have to really solve
the Schrodinger equation.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.220
But for the force interaction
due to the [? charge, ?]

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:44.120
direct, [INAUDIBLE] charge
interaction Coulombic force,

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:49.340
you can write starting with
the [? point-potential ?]

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:53.090
construct, [? with ?] what if
you have two positive-negative

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:53.900
charge together?

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:55.070
That's a dipole.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:58.760
And then you have one-point
charge to a dipole.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.160
And you have dipole to dipole.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.890
And you can do dipole
to semi-infinite object.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:08.360
And finally, we give an
expression for example.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.560
What's the force the Van
der Waals force between--

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.470
that's the dipole-based model,
dipole interaction between two

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:24.740
semi and infinite [? plates. ?]
And the potential is inversely

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:32.060
proportional to the separation,
D, between these two surfaces.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:35.380
And this potential is--

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:38.780
you can say when
you go to smaller D,

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:40.610
it goes to more negative.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:44.090
So it's attractive
potential and so attractive

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:47.480
force between the two surfaces.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.260
And on the top,
the constant here

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:54.410
is also called Hamaker constant.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.960
And it has-- it's
a physical region.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:00.140
If you learn, say, if
you do a phase change,

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:05.030
you'll probably say this has
a Hamaker constant before.

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:09.140
And [INAUDIBLE] that's the
Casimir force is dipole-dipole.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:13.740
This Van der Waals force is
dipole-dipole interaction.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:18.170
And the extension of that
is the Casimir force.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:22.580
And the Casimir force
is, as I explained,

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:30.470
it was the idea that the
force between the two object,

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:36.440
1 and 2, and can be think
about that the energy stored

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:40.410
in this space between
1 and 2, the medium--

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.560
that's right--
could be a vacuum.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.840
And when I think
about energy stored

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:49.880
and there is a zero-point, we
learned that the quantization

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:51.440
one half h nu.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.670
And then how many modes you
have, electromagnetic modes,

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.150
in this space?

00:07:57.150 --> 00:08:00.710
And if each mode can turn
energy one half h nu,

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.740
you do that summation
of the total energy.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.110
And you find out that
either of the two surface

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.690
gets closer to
each other, you're

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.330
excluding modes so the
energy is decreasing.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.530
And so you have
attractive force.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:20.210
So that gave the quantum spin
to the Van der Waals force

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.100
interaction picture.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.470
And that was [? a ?]
[? Casamirs. ?] Very simple

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:25.970
paper.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:27.980
If you are interested,
you can read it.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:32.120
And then there was the
extension by Lifshitz,

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.400
the Landau-Lifshitz book.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.210
But Lifshitz is
solving this problem

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:41.270
from electromagnetic dynamics
point of view, basically

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:47.990
[INAUDIBLE] consider the
force interaction, as I say,

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:52.010
due to the zero
point [? jiggling. ?]

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:53.790
So zero point
[? jiggling ?] means what?

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.340
There's no temperature
there, right,

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:58.080
so any object is uncertain.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.870
So the position is not fixed.

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.660
So zero point has a fluctuation.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:06.650
And this fluctuating will
generate electromagnetic wave.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:10.700
The electromagnetic waves
propagate to this side.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:14.210
And that will create
an interaction

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:15.560
between the two sides.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:19.610
So you basically [? solve ?]
Maxwell's equation.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.750
And then the jiggling
is, again, connected

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:23.500
with the so-called
fluctuation-dissipation

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:29.950
theorem, so you can calculate
the force interaction

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:33.140
from electrodynamics
perspective.

00:09:33.140 --> 00:09:36.140
And that's a much
more generous theory.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:38.710
So what you have is 1, 2, 3.

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:42.340
And what you need is the
[? dielectric ?] constant,

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:45.160
epsilon 1, which could be
a function of frequency

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:47.710
and epsilon 2, which could
be a function of frequency,

00:09:47.710 --> 00:09:50.810
and epsilon 3, which could
be a function of frequency.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:54.370
So with that, you can
calculate the force interaction

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:55.660
in any medium.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:10:00.190
Sometimes, the two medium
in between are not vacuum;

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:03.790
it could be another material.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:07.480
And in fact, there is a--

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.280
in myself even have--

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.440
a little bit confusion here.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.710
This concept, again,
if you do phase change,

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:17.320
you'll probably encounter--
how many of you have

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.630
heard of disjoining pressure?

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:21.900
Anybody has heard about that?

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.310
OK, yeah.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.100
Disjoining pressure is,
again, another concept

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:30.430
that is encountered in
the phase change process.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:37.080
For example, if you consider the
evaporation of a liquid film,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.920
so you have a corner, and the
liquid the film will spread.

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:45.000
And, of course, there is
a surface tension part.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.950
But there could be another
part, say a very, very thin film

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:54.010
spreading much larger distance.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:58.730
And people say that's because
of disjoining pressure.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:03.010
And the reason for
that is you can think

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:05.050
about this as a medium one.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:06.610
A vacuum is a medium, remember.

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:11.140
Or say any space for
electromagnetic wave

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:12.100
is a medium.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.980
And that the liquid
film is medium 3.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:18.850
And whatever there is solid,
there is a medium too.

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:21.850
So I can calculate
the three medium.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:24.820
What then, say, what's
the interaction?

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:30.010
And it turns out that in
some tests, most of the case

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:31.570
this is attractive.

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:33.820
In some cases there,
the force in between

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:36.670
could also be repulsive.

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:39.700
If it's a repulsive
force, then there actually

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:44.740
will be a stable
layer of liquid that's

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.030
sustained on the surface.

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:50.960
If it's evaporation,
if it's a heating,

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:54.020
then if you have a liquid film,
you help your heat transfer.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:59.300
So there is this
disjointing pressure

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:01.250
that people sometimes use.

00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:05.180
I think if anyone is interested
in understanding this,

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:09.230
you should look more
into the literature.

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:14.690
So this is the
picture that we have

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:16.850
in terms of the
attractive force.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:21.770
Where we're going is talking
about, as I mentioned before,

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:25.160
is I have a suspension, I
have particles in the liquid.

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:28.170
And the [? widest ?] suspension,
I can sometimes make this

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:32.120
stable. [? Why ?] sometimes
I can't make it stable?

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:35.630
And of course, if
it's all attractive,

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:39.170
Van der Waals force, then
I will not have stability,

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:40.730
because the particle
eventually glue

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:44.130
to each other and [? sediment ?]
because of gravity.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:46.445
So what I [? leave ?]
is a repulsive force.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:53.330
[INAUDIBLE] [? say ?]
[? common ?] repulsive force,

00:12:53.330 --> 00:12:57.940
particularly in ionic
solution, like say, water,

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.400
is the electric double layer.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:03.202
So let's spend a little
bit of time discussing

00:13:03.202 --> 00:13:04.285
the electric double layer.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:27.490
So a lot of solid surfaces,
when you immersing

00:13:27.490 --> 00:13:33.460
in a solution, the
[INAUDIBLE] the [? surface, ?]

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.100
contains charge.

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:39.650
And you can think
about, for example,

00:13:39.650 --> 00:13:44.020
if I have oxygen
[? turbulation ?]

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:50.710
on the surface, if I put in
water, what could be happening?

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:53.860
You think this will be a
hydrophilic or hydrophobic

00:13:53.860 --> 00:13:56.140
surface?

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:58.090
Does it like water, or
it doesn't like water?

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:09.180
It will be hydrophilic most
likely, because most likely,

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:14.340
the surface has oxygen.
Oxygen is a negative charge.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:18.675
And the water has a hydrogen.
And the hydrogen and the oxygen

00:14:18.675 --> 00:14:21.060
will bind to each other.

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:24.540
And so a lot of surface actually
have a [INAUDIBLE] surface

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:29.760
charge, either because of the
asymmetry of the bonds because

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.100
on the surface, atom is
different from internal.

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:35.400
You don't have the surrounding
atoms to balance it.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:39.600
Or, it's just because it just
absorbed some ions onto it

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:42.210
to have a [INAUDIBLE] [? so ?]
[? a ?] [INAUDIBLE] charge

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:43.570
on the surface.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:46.380
So you have on
the solid surface,

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:56.130
typically, let's say here,
I'm drawing just the inside.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:02.430
I got a net balance of charge
on the surface, negative charge.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:05.430
And now, if I
emerged in a solution

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:08.100
that has positive
and negative ions,

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.980
and then you know
that the positive ions

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:15.030
will go towards the
surface just to balance it.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:19.500
So what happens is
some of these ions

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:23.790
will be pretty much
glued because the bond is

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:24.780
strong enough.

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:28.710
So the first monolayer will
pretty much be like glued

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:31.090
on to the surface.

00:15:31.090 --> 00:15:35.250
And once you are, say,
one monolayer away,

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:39.180
the first interaction
decays rapidly,

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:43.210
and the bonding will not be
strong enough to hold them.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:50.070
So this other counter ions
will move around the surface.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:52.160
But say because of
the charge imbalance,

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:58.500
so there will be a [? let ?]
more charge mobile ions

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:02.510
in this region in the liquid.

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:07.180
So what we will create
is in the surface region

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:08.960
like a capacitor.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:15.500
On the say solid side, you
have one type of charged ions,

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:19.850
and on the liquid side, you
got another type of ions.

00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:23.850
This forms electrical
double layer.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:28.370
And in fact, if you
define more carefully

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:35.450
[? and ?] the immobile layer
of ions is a Stern layer.

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:37.940
It's called a Stern test.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:44.280
OK.

