00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:45.745
Surface tension.

00:55:48.280 --> 00:55:52.280
So what is surface tension?

00:55:52.280 --> 00:55:55.490
First question is, what's
a unit of surface tension?

00:56:03.260 --> 00:56:05.660
Newton per meter.

00:56:05.660 --> 00:56:08.270
So let's look at,
understand a little bit more

00:56:08.270 --> 00:56:09.305
on surface tension.

00:56:20.270 --> 00:56:23.040
Surface tension is the energy.

00:56:23.040 --> 00:56:26.920
So first, the unit
is newton per meter.

00:56:29.630 --> 00:56:32.450
You think of it
typically as a force,

00:56:32.450 --> 00:56:34.880
surface tension as a force.

00:56:34.880 --> 00:56:37.610
But then, I can write
this newton times

00:56:37.610 --> 00:56:41.120
meter per meter squared.

00:56:41.120 --> 00:56:44.870
That's [? joule ?]
per meter squared.

00:56:44.870 --> 00:56:46.340
What is it?

00:56:46.340 --> 00:56:49.240
It's a surface energy.

00:56:49.240 --> 00:56:52.900
So the surface tension
is how much energy

00:56:52.900 --> 00:56:55.030
I need to create a surface.

00:56:59.340 --> 00:57:03.580
And we can start with
the same material.

00:57:03.580 --> 00:57:12.020
So if I have material
one I want to break it.

00:57:12.020 --> 00:57:17.450
What's the minimum energy I
need to break the surface?

00:57:17.450 --> 00:57:19.730
The minimum energy
I [? need ?] is

00:57:19.730 --> 00:57:26.300
once I break into two halves, I
got two exposed surfaces there.

00:57:29.620 --> 00:57:37.100
So the surface energy is then
the surface energy [? comma ?]

00:57:37.100 --> 00:57:42.230
one is then half of
the minimal work I

00:57:42.230 --> 00:57:44.820
need to do to create this
two [INAUDIBLE] surfaces.

00:57:47.870 --> 00:57:50.790
So that's the cohesive energy.

00:57:50.790 --> 00:57:53.030
It's the same material.

00:57:53.030 --> 00:57:54.780
How much energy do you need?

00:57:54.780 --> 00:57:56.030
It's a cohesive energy.

00:57:58.545 --> 00:57:59.045
Cohesion.

00:58:04.340 --> 00:58:08.780
And W here is the
work of cohesion.

00:58:08.780 --> 00:58:13.040
And so this is one case.

00:58:13.040 --> 00:58:20.600
Now, if I have two different
material, 1 and then 2,

00:58:20.600 --> 00:58:24.330
how much energy do I
need to break and create

00:58:24.330 --> 00:58:26.540
two different surfaces?

00:58:26.540 --> 00:58:28.760
Material 1 and material 2.

00:58:32.390 --> 00:58:34.460
And this is the
interface between two

00:58:34.460 --> 00:58:36.170
different materials.

00:58:36.170 --> 00:58:41.940
That's my surface tension
between the two materials.

00:58:41.940 --> 00:58:46.430
So from an energy perspective,
it's much easier to understand.

00:58:46.430 --> 00:58:47.990
And my surface tension--

00:58:56.750 --> 00:58:59.320
so surface tension
exists for any surface--

00:58:59.320 --> 00:59:01.580
not only liquid, but also solid.

00:59:01.580 --> 00:59:06.110
And the surface tension
in this case, r 1 2,

00:59:06.110 --> 00:59:14.810
can be expressed in terms
of the surface energy,

00:59:14.810 --> 00:59:23.930
r1, plus r2 minus 2 times r.

00:59:23.930 --> 00:59:27.180
So here, I only look at
the Van der Waals part.

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:31.040
So Van der Waals
part of this break--

00:59:31.040 --> 00:59:34.100
how much Van der
Waals surface energy

00:59:34.100 --> 00:59:38.420
if I break up surface 1 only,
and I break up surface 2 only?

00:59:44.440 --> 00:59:52.060
So this gives us some ways to
estimate the surface energy,

00:59:52.060 --> 00:59:52.990
surface tension.

